---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
---

<Layout
  title="About ShouldISpend"
  description="Learn what Should I Spend does, how its calculators are built, how to interpret results, and why the site focuses on practical spending decisions."
>
  <style>
    .about-page {
      max-width: 980px;
      margin: 0 auto;
      padding: 82px 24px 24px;
    }

    .eyebrow {
      color: var(--primary);
      text-transform: uppercase;
      letter-spacing: 1.8px;
      font-size: 0.78rem;
      font-weight: 800;
      margin-bottom: 16px;
    }

    h1 {
      max-width: 850px;
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: clamp(2.8rem, 6vw, 5rem);
      line-height: 0.98;
      letter-spacing: -0.05em;
      margin: 0 0 24px;
    }

    h2 {
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: clamp(1.8rem, 3vw, 2.55rem);
      line-height: 1.08;
      letter-spacing: -0.035em;
      margin: 0 0 18px;
    }

    p,
    li {
      color: var(--muted);
      font-size: 1.06rem;
      line-height: 1.85;
    }

    p {
      margin: 0 0 22px;
    }

    a {
      color: var(--primary);
      font-weight: 800;
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    a:hover {
      text-decoration: underline;
    }

    .intro {
      max-width: 820px;
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: 1.22rem;
      line-height: 1.75;
    }

    .panel {
      background: var(--card);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 24px;
      padding: 30px;
      margin-top: 34px;
      box-shadow: var(--shadow-soft);
    }

    .section {
      margin-top: 58px;
    }

    .grid {
      display: grid;
      grid-template-columns: repeat(auto-fit, minmax(240px, 1fr));
      gap: 22px;
      margin-top: 24px;
    }

    .card {
      background: var(--card);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
      border-radius: 20px;
      padding: 24px;
    }

    .card h3 {
      color: var(--text);
      font-size: 1.18rem;
      line-height: 1.3;
      margin: 0 0 12px;
    }

    .card p {
      font-size: 0.98rem;
      line-height: 1.75;
      margin: 0;
    }

    .founder {
      background: var(--primary-dark);
      border-color: var(--primary-dark);
      color: #ffffff;
    }

    .founder h2 {
      color: #ffffff;
    }

    .founder p {
      color: #e7eadf;
    }

    .signature {
      color: #ffffff;
      font-weight: 800;
      margin-top: 6px;
    }

    .cta {
      display: flex;
      flex-wrap: wrap;
      gap: 12px;
      margin-top: 24px;
    }

    .button,
    .button-secondary {
      display: inline-flex;
      align-items: center;
      justify-content: center;
      border-radius: 999px;
      padding: 12px 20px;
      font-weight: 800;
      text-decoration: none;
    }

    .button {
      background: var(--primary);
      color: #ffffff;
      border: 1px solid var(--primary);
    }

    .button-secondary {
      background: var(--card);
      color: var(--primary);
      border: 1px solid var(--border);
    }

    .button:hover,
    .button-secondary:hover {
      text-decoration: none;
      transform: translateY(-2px);
    }

    @media (max-width: 700px) {
      .about-page {
        padding: 58px 20px 12px;
      }

      .panel,
      .card {
        padding: 22px;
      }

      .intro {
        font-size: 1.08rem;
      }

      .button,
      .button-secondary {
        width: 100%;
      }
    }

    .trust-closeout-section {
      margin-top: 32px;
      padding-top: 28px;
      border-top: 1px solid var(--border);
    }

    .trust-closeout-section h2 {
      color: var(--text);
      margin-bottom: 14px;
    }

    .trust-closeout-section p {
      color: var(--muted);
      line-height: 1.75;
      margin: 0 0 16px;
    }

  </style>

  <div class="about-page">
    <section>
      <div class="eyebrow">About ShouldISpend</div>

      <h1>About Should I Spend</h1>

      <p class="intro">
        ShouldISpend helps people pressure-test the spending decisions that look simple
        until real life gets involved: rent, groceries, weddings, medical bills, cars,
        college, baby costs, funerals, travel, debt, and emergency savings.
      </p>

      <div class="panel">
        <h2>Reviewed and maintained by the site owner.</h2>
        <p>
          <strong>Written and maintained by Dustin Baker.</strong> Last reviewed: July 2026.
          ShouldISpend is maintained through regular reviews of calculator accuracy, clear ownership and methodology, working links, practical examples, and uncommon scenarios across the calculator library.
        </p>
        <p>
          The site is independent and educational. It is built to help readers pressure-test spending
          decisions before money leaves their account, not to sell financial products or replace
          individualized professional advice.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="panel founder">
        <h2>Why I built ShouldISpend</h2>
        <p>
          I built ShouldISpend for the kind of money decisions that do not fit neatly
          inside a generic budgeting rule. A wedding, a car payment, a medical bill,
          a baby, a funeral - the question is not just whether you can pay for it today.
          It is whether it still feels manageable after the money is gone.
        </p>
        <p>
          I wanted a site that gives a straight verdict instead of a lecture. Not a perfect
          financial plan. Not a magic answer. Just a clearer way to see the tradeoff before
          you commit to something big.
        </p>
        <div class="signature">Dustin Baker, founder</div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="eyebrow">How the site thinks</div>
      <h2>Price is only one part of the decision.</h2>

      <p>
        A $700 car payment can be fine for one household and a problem for another.
        A $30,000 wedding can be manageable in one situation and reckless in another.
        The number alone does not answer the question.
      </p>

      <p>
        That is why ShouldISpend calculators look at the surrounding context: income,
        savings, debt, monthly obligations, outside help, emergency cushion, payment plans,
        and what the decision leaves behind.
      </p>

      <div class="grid">
        <div class="card">
          <h3>Real-life inputs</h3>
          <p>
            The calculators are built around the details people actually juggle:
            income, savings, debt, family help, monthly bills, and the cost in front of them.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Straight verdicts</h3>
          <p>
            Results are designed to be clear: go, wait, rethink it, or proceed carefully
            based on the pressure the decision creates.
          </p>
        </div>

        <div class="card">
          <h3>Conservative by design</h3>
          <p>
            If a decision leaves too little cushion, the site is supposed to say so.
            A cautious warning before you spend is better than regret after.
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </section>

    <section class="section">
      <div class="eyebrow">What ShouldISpend is not</div>
      <h2>This is educational decision support, not financial advice.</h2>

      <p>
        ShouldISpend does not replace a financial advisor, tax professional, attorney,
        lender, doctor, insurance expert, or anyone who can evaluate your full personal
        situation. The calculators are educational tools built to help you think more
        clearly before making a spending decision.
      </p>

      <p>
        The goal is not to tell everyone to spend less. The goal is to show whether the
        decision fits your actual situation, what tradeoff it creates, and whether you
        still have enough room after the money leaves.
      </p>

      <div class="cta">
        <a class="button" href="/calculators/">Browse Calculators</a>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/methodology/">See the Methodology &rarr;</a>
        <a class="button-secondary" href="/editorial-policy/">Editorial Policy &rarr;</a>
      </div>
    </section>
  </div>

      <section id="TrustAboutCloseout" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>What Should I Spend is built to do</h2>
        <p>
          Should I Spend is a practical decision-support site for everyday money
          questions. The goal is not to make a decision for the reader. The goal is to
          slow the decision down, organize the real costs, and show what might happen to
          cash flow, savings, debt pressure, and short-term risk after the purchase or
          life event.
        </p>
        <p>
          The calculators are written for situations where the headline price is not the
          full decision: moving out, renting, groceries, college, funeral costs, car
          costs, weddings, medical bills, baby expenses, travel, and other household
          tradeoffs. Those decisions often involve deposits, monthly commitments, timing,
          fees, insurance, supplies, travel, emergency savings, and the cost of being
          wrong.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>How readers should use the calculators</h2>
        <p>
          A calculator result should be treated as a planning estimate, not a command.
          Readers should enter realistic numbers, compare the result with their actual
          budget, and then verify quotes, due dates, lease terms, billing rules, and
          required fees before paying. When the calculator shows a close call, the safer
          next step is usually to reduce the cost, delay the purchase, build more
          cushion, or compare alternatives.
        </p>
        <p>
          The site is intentionally conservative around cash cushion because many
          spending decisions fail after the bill is paid. A move, wedding, funeral,
          school decision, medical bill, or vehicle expense can be technically affordable
          on day one but still create stress if it leaves no money for groceries,
          transportation, utilities, debt payments, or one ordinary surprise.
        </p>
      </section>

      <section class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>How the site approaches quality</h2>
        <p>
          Pages are written to explain what belongs in the estimate, what can change the
          answer, when the result is not enough, and what a reader should check before
          making a final decision. The calculators are not built as bare math widgets.
          They are meant to pair the math with plain-language judgment, examples, risk
          checks, and next steps.
        </p>
        <p>
          Should I Spend does not replace a financial advisor, attorney, tax
          professional, doctor, insurance agent, landlord, lender, or benefits office.
          It provides structured consumer education so readers can ask better questions
          before committing money.
        </p>
      </section>


      <section id="TrustAboutCloseoutUseCase" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Why the site emphasizes context before a decision</h2>
        <p>
          Many spending decisions look simple because the first price is visible and the
          follow-up costs are scattered. A reader might know the rent but not the move-in
          cash, know the truck price but not the mileage and fuel, or know the medical
          bill but not the payment-plan tradeoff. Should I Spend is designed to make
          those hidden pieces easier to see before money changes hands.
        </p>
        <p>
          The most useful result is often not a yes or no. It is a clearer next step:
          gather a quote, check a policy, wait for one more paycheck, lower the upfront
          cost, compare a smaller option, or protect the emergency cushion before
          committing.
        </p>
      </section>


      <section id="TrustAboutCloseoutReaderFit" class="trust-closeout-section">
        <h2>Who the site is written for</h2>
        <p>
          The site is written for readers who need a practical spending check before
          making a commitment. That may include a renter comparing move-out costs, a
          parent estimating baby expenses, a household planning groceries, a student
          weighing school costs, or a family trying to understand funeral or medical
          bills. The common thread is the same: the decision has more than one cost, and
          the reader needs a clearer way to organize it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Should I Spend is most useful when the reader has real numbers to test and is
          willing to verify the final terms before paying. It is less useful when the
          reader needs personalized professional advice, emergency help, or a final
          answer that depends on private facts the site cannot review.
        </p>
      </section>

      </Layout>
